---
// The winner's curse, in plain English, then measured on the page's own seeded dashboards. Every
// number below comes from measureWinnersCurse (src/lib/stats-sim/winners-curse.ts), run once at
// build time by the page and passed in, and pinned in tests/stats-sim/winners-curse.test.ts.
// Nothing is typed in by hand. Astro drops a line break that touches a tag or an {expression}, so
// the prose below only breaks lines between plain words.
import { formatCount, formatLift, formatShare, type Rule, type WinnersCurseResult } from "../../../lib/stats-sim/winners-curse";

interface Props {
	curse: WinnersCurseResult;
}

const { curse } = Astro.props;
const { config } = curse;
const points = (x: number | null) => (x === null ? "—" : (x * 100).toFixed(2));

const liftedPerDashboard = config.num_metrics - Math.round(config.num_metrics * config.fraction_null);
const labels: Record<Rule, string> = {
	none: "Flagged, no correction",
	bh: "Flagged, Benjamini-Hochberg",
	bonferroni: "Flagged, Bonferroni",
};
const rows = (["none", "bh", "bonferroni"] as const).map((rule) => ({ rule, label: labels[rule], ...curse.rules[rule] }));
const { none, bh, bonferroni } = curse.rules;
---

<section id="winners-curse" class="mx-auto max-w-7xl scroll-mt-24 px-6 py-16 md:px-12 md:py-20">
	<p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-accent-blue">Trap two</p>
	<h2 class="mt-2 font-anton text-4xl md:text-5xl">Why do real winners look bigger than they are?</h2>
	<p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-ink">
		The <strong>winner's curse</strong> got its name in the oil business. Companies bidding on federal offshore leases
		in the Gulf of Mexico each estimated how much oil sat under a tract. Some guessed high, some low, and on average
		the guesses could be fair. But whoever won an auction was, almost by definition, the bidder who guessed highest,
		so the winners kept overpaying. Capen, Clapp and Campbell wrote it up in 1971, and the name stuck: winning
		selects the biggest overestimate.
	</p>
	<p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
		A dashboard runs that same auction on every real lift. Each measurement is the true lift plus some noise,
		sometimes pushing it up, sometimes down. The significance bar is the auction: the real lifts that clear it are,
		disproportionately, the ones whose noise happened to push them up. So when a real winner gets flagged, its
		measured lift is a guess that won a contest for being high. Gelman and Carlin call that overshoot a Type M, for
		magnitude, error.
	</p>
	<p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
		That makes it a different trap from the first one, asking a different question. Multiple testing asks whether a
		flagged winner is real at all. The winner's curse asks how big a real winner is. Same flags, two different ways
		to be wrong, and the corrections from the first half only fix the first: a stricter bar lets through only the
		luckiest draws, so the real winners it does flag come out even more exaggerated.
	</p>

	<figure class="mt-10 raw-box rounded-xl bg-cream p-6">
		<figcaption class="font-display text-lg font-semibold">Is it real? How big is it? The same flags, both questions</figcaption>
		<p class="mt-1 max-w-[65ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
			The live experiment's dashboard, run {formatCount(curse.dashboards)} times, each rule applied to every run.
		</p>
		<div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto">
			<table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
				<thead>
					<tr class="border-b border-line align-bottom font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-soft">
						<th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-3 font-normal md:pr-4">Which metrics</th>
						<th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-3 font-normal md:pr-4">
							<span class="block text-accent-blue">Is it real?</span>
							Flags on a null metric
						</th>
						<th scope="col" class="py-2 font-normal">
							<span class="block text-accent-blue">How big is it?</span>
							Average observed lift
						</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr class="border-b border-line/60 align-top">
						<th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-3 font-normal text-ink-soft md:pr-4">Every real lift, flagged or not</th>
						<td class="py-2 pr-3 font-mono text-ink-soft md:pr-4">&mdash;</td>
						<td class="py-2 font-mono">
							{formatLift(curse.mean_observed_lift_all)}
							<span class="block text-xs text-ink-soft">{formatCount(curse.lifted_metrics)} metrics</span>
						</td>
					</tr>
					{
						rows.map((row) => (
							<tr class="border-b border-line/60 align-top">
								<th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-3 font-normal text-ink md:pr-4">
									{row.label}
								</th>
								<td class="py-2 pr-3 font-mono md:pr-4">
									{formatShare(row.null_share)}
									<span class="block text-xs text-ink-soft">
										{formatCount(row.null_flags)} of {formatCount(row.flags)} flags
									</span>
								</td>
								<td class="py-2 font-mono">
									{formatLift(row.mean_observed_lift)}
									<span class="block text-xs text-ink-soft">{formatCount(row.lifted_flags)} real winners</span>
								</td>
							</tr>
						))
					}
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p class="mt-5 max-w-[65ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
			Read down the columns. Each stricter rule cuts the fake flags, from {formatShare(none.null_share)} to {formatShare(bh.null_share)} to {formatShare(bonferroni.null_share)}, and
			pushes the real winners' average further from the truth, from {formatLift(none.mean_observed_lift)} to {formatLift(bh.mean_observed_lift)} to {formatLift(bonferroni.mean_observed_lift)}, on a
			true lift of {formatLift(curse.true_lift)}. The same bar that fixes false discoveries makes the curse worse.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-xs leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
			{formatCount(curse.dashboards)} simulated dashboards, seeds {formatCount(curse.first_seed)}–{formatCount(curse.last_seed)}, at the
			live experiment's defaults: {config.num_metrics} metrics, {liftedPerDashboard} of them lifted by a true {formatLift(curse.true_lift)} on a {Math.round(config.baseline_rate * 100)}% baseline, {formatCount(config.visitors_per_arm)} visitors per
			arm, α = {curse.alpha}. The yardstick is {formatLift(curse.mean_observed_lift_all)}, not {formatLift(curse.true_lift)}, and
			that first gap isn't the curse: a ratio of two noisy rates runs high on average even when nothing is selected.
			Measured as a plain difference in rates, which doesn't, every lifted metric averages {points(curse.mean_observed_diff_all)} percentage
			points against a true {points(curse.true_diff)}. Computed at build time by src/lib/stats-sim/winners-curse.ts and
			pinned in tests/stats-sim/winners-curse.test.ts.
		</p>
	</figure>

	<div class="mt-10 raw-box rounded-xl bg-cream p-6">
		<p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-accent-blue">The fix isn't a stricter bar</p>
		<p class="mt-2 max-w-[65ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
			Two things actually shrink it. More traffic: once nearly every real lift clears the bar, clearing it stops
			selecting for luck, the study further down shows how fast that happens. And measuring a winner again on fresh
			traffic, a holdout or a re-test, since nothing about the second measurement was selected. Re-run on fresh
			traffic, Bonferroni's {formatLift(bonferroni.mean_observed_lift)} winners come back at {formatLift(bonferroni.mean_retest_lift)}, right on the unselected
			average. Shrinkage, pulling flagged estimates back toward what's typical, does a similar job without new traffic,
			but it needs a prior built from many past experiments, which this page doesn't model.
		</p>
	</div>
</section>
